{
 "id": "2019-gao-urban",
 "name": "Urban Green Space Structure and Landscape Preference",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the photo-elicitation condition with the original photographs: three small urban green spaces in China (the paper does not name the sites), photographed in early spring and differing in the horizontal structure of their vegetation, open (less than 10% tree and shrub canopy), semi-open (30-70%) and closed (more than 70%). Each participant is randomly assigned to one green space and sees its three photos as a slide show, each photo for 20 s, the whole set shown twice (as in the paper's slide projection). They then answer the paper's questionnaire: gender, whether they study landscape architecture, preference for the green space on a 5-point scale (1 strongly dislike - 5 strongly like), which recreational activities they would like to do there (the paper's 12 activities in three groups: physical - playing ball games, flying kites, practicing martial arts, walking; social - picnicking, gatherings, chatting, playing games; mental - emptying, meditating, sitting quietly, thinking) and a free comment. Rebuilt from Gao, Liang, Chen & Qiu (2019), Comparisons of Landscape Preferences through Three Different Perceptual Approaches, International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health 16(23): 4754, with the nine photos cropped from its Figure 1 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: open_1-3, semi_open_1-3, closed_1-3; green_space records the assignment. Analysis as in the paper: GLM of preference on green-space type, gender and professional background with Duncan post hoc tests, and counts of chosen activities by type; the paper (180 students, 20 per group) found the semi-open space preferred most, then closed, then open, no gender or professional effects, and walking the most chosen activity (45.5%). Differences from the original study: (1) only the photo condition is rebuilt; the on-site visit and the VR-headset panorama conditions of the paper are not included; (2) the question wording, the gloss '(the mind)' after 'emptying', the 'none' option for activities and the comment prompt are ours (the paper lists the content but does not print the questions); (3) the paper's participants were college students in a classroom; anyone can take part here.",
